---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
const desc = 'Free three-phase amperage calculator for data center racks: current draw at 208V, 415V and 480V, breaker size at the 80% continuous rule, and how many racks a circuit supports.';
const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication',
  name: 'Three-Phase Amperage Calculator', applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Web', offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  url: 'https://theworldofai.org/calculators/three-phase/',
});

const faqs = [
  { q: 'How many amps does a 12 kW rack draw at 208V three-phase?',
    a: 'About 33.6 amps. The arithmetic is 12,000 watts divided by 208 volts times the square root of three times a power factor of 0.99, which is 12,000 divided by about 357. On a 60 amp breaker, derated to 48 amps for continuous load, that is one rack per circuit with 14 amps to spare.' },
  { q: 'Why is a breaker only good for 80 percent of its rating?',
    a: 'Because a load running three hours or more is treated as continuous, and continuous loads are limited to 80 percent of the overcurrent device rating under US electrical code convention. A data center rack is continuous by definition, so a 60 amp circuit is a 48 amp circuit for planning purposes.' },
  { q: 'Where does the square root of three come from?',
    a: 'From the geometry of a balanced three-phase system. The three phases are 120 degrees apart, so line-to-line voltage is the square root of three times line-to-neutral voltage, and the total power relationship carries the same factor. It is why three-phase delivers more power over the same conductors than single phase.' },
  { q: 'Why are dense racks moving to 415V?',
    a: 'Because current falls as voltage rises for the same power, and current is what sizes conductors, breakers and busway. The same 12 kW that draws 33.6 amps at 208V draws about 16.8 at 415V, which halves the copper and roughly quarters the resistive loss in distribution. It is the cheapest available efficiency gain in a high-density design.' },
  { q: 'Should I plan A and B feeds at half load each?',
    a: 'No, and this is the common and expensive error. In a dual-corded 2N design each feed must be able to carry the entire rack load, because the whole point is that either one can fail. Sizing each at half means the survivor trips during the event the redundancy existed for. Design each feed for full load and expect each to run near half in normal operation.' },
];
---
<Base title="Three-Phase Amperage Calculator for Data Center Racks — The World of AI" description={desc} current="/calculators/" jsonld={jsonld}>
  <h1>Three-Phase Amperage Calculator</h1>
  <p class="answer">Current on a three-phase circuit is power divided by voltage times the square root of three times power factor. Because continuous loads are limited to 80% of breaker rating by code convention, the breaker you specify is always larger than the current you calculate, and that 80% rule is what decides how many racks a circuit really supports.</p>
  <p class="meta-line">Formula and defaults last reviewed: 2026-08-31.</p>

  <div class="calc">
    <label>Rack or circuit load (kW) <input type="number" id="kw" value="12" step="0.5" min="0" /></label>
    <label>Voltage
      <select id="v">
        <option value="208" selected>208V three-phase (US common)</option>
        <option value="415">415V three-phase (400/415V, higher density)</option>
        <option value="480">480V three-phase (distribution)</option>
        <option value="400">400V three-phase (IEC)</option>
      </select>
    </label>
    <label>Power factor <input type="number" id="pf" value="0.99" step="0.01" min="0.5" /></label>
    <label>Breaker rating available (A) <input type="number" id="brk" value="60" step="5" min="5" /></label>
    <div class="out" id="out" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>

  <h2>How the math works</h2>
  <p>Amps = (kW × 1000) ÷ (volts × √3 × power factor). The √3 comes from the phase relationship
    in a balanced three-phase system. Power factor near 1.0 is realistic for modern server power
    supplies with active correction; older or lightly loaded equipment sits lower and draws more
    current for the same real power.</p>
  <h2>What this does not tell you</h2>
  <p>This assumes balanced phases. Real cabinets rarely balance perfectly, and the phase carrying
    the most current is the one that trips, so per-phase metering matters more than the average
    this produces. Voltage drop over long runs, harmonic content and derating for ambient
    temperature or conduit fill are all real and none of them are modelled here.</p>

  <h2>What does a 12 kW rack need at 208V?</h2>
  <p>Amps are 12,000 divided by the product of 208 volts, the square root of three, and a power
    factor of 0.99. That product is about 357, so the draw is about 33.6 amps.</p>
  <p>Now apply the continuous-load rule. A 60 amp breaker is good for 48 amps continuously, so one
    12 kW rack fits with 14 amps unused, and a second would need 67 amps and does not fit. The
    circuit supports one rack, and the 26 percent of the breaker left idle is the cost of the
    safety margin, not spare capacity to plan against.</p>
  <p>At 415V the same rack draws about 16.8 amps, so two fit on the same 48 amp usable budget with
    room left. That is the whole argument for higher distribution voltage in one comparison.</p>

  <h2>What actually limits racks per circuit?</h2>
  <p>Three things in sequence, and the first to bind wins. The 80 percent rule caps usable current
    below the breaker rating. Whole racks do not divide evenly into what remains, so a circuit
    frequently strands capacity that cannot be given to anything: 48 usable amps against a 33.6
    amp rack strands 14 amps, or 29 percent, and no amount of arithmetic recovers it. Then
    redundancy doubles the installed circuit count, because A and B feeds each carry full load.</p>
  <p>The consequence for planning is that installed electrical capacity always exceeds the sum of
    rack nameplates by a wide margin, and that the gap is structural rather than wasteful. A
    facility quoting critical IT capacity and one quoting installed switchgear capacity are not
    describing the same number, and the ratio between them is set here.</p>

  <Faq items={faqs} />

  <div class="cite-box">Cite this tool: <code>"Three-Phase Amperage Calculator." The World of AI,
    theworldofai.org/calculators/three-phase/.</code></div>

  <script is:inline>
  (function () {
    function n(i) { return parseFloat(document.getElementById(i).value) || 0; }
    function f(v, d) { return v.toLocaleString(undefined, { maximumFractionDigits: d === undefined ? 0 : d }); }
    var IDS = ['kw','v','pf','brk'];
    function calc() {
      var amps = (n('kw') * 1000) / (n('v') * Math.sqrt(3) * n('pf'));
      var need = amps / 0.8;
      var usable = n('brk') * 0.8;
      var perCircuit = amps > 0 ? Math.floor(usable / amps) : 0;
      document.getElementById('out').innerHTML =
        'Current draw: <b>' + f(amps, 1) + ' A per phase</b><br>' +
        'Minimum breaker at the 80% continuous rule: <b>' + f(need, 1) + ' A</b><br>' +
        'A ' + f(n('brk')) + 'A breaker gives ' + f(usable, 1) + 'A usable, supporting <b>' + perCircuit + '</b> load(s) of this size<br>' +
        '<span class="sub">Higher voltage means lower current for the same power, which is why dense halls move to 415V: thinner conductors, less copper, less loss.</span>';
    }
    IDS.forEach(function (i) {
      document.getElementById(i).addEventListener('input', calc);
      document.getElementById(i).addEventListener('change', calc);
    });
    calc();
  })();
  </script>
  <style>
    .calc { border: 1px solid #e6e4de; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; background: #fafaf6; margin: 1.4em 0; }
    .calc label { display: block; margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 14px; color: #26262b; }
    .calc input, .calc select { display: block; margin-top: 4px; padding: 7px 9px; font-size: 15px;
      border: 1px solid #cfcdc6; border-radius: 6px; width: 220px; max-width: 100%; }
    .out { margin-top: 14px; padding-top: 14px; border-top: 1px solid #e6e4de; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.7; }
    .out b { color: #201868; }
    .sub { color: #7A8A9E; font-size: 13.5px; }
  </style>
</Base>
